## 2026-08-27 — Menu tabs: one shared Back button, top-left, red

- **What**: The "leave this screen" button was in a different place on every
  menu tab — top-left in PlayGameScreen (in the title HBox), bottom-right in
  TrainingSetupScreen (anchored), bottom-centre in PreGameLobby (in the flow
  VBox). Also three different labels ("← Back", "Back", "← Leave") and two
  different button styles. Unified on **top-left, red, "Back"** (Abe's call).
- **How**: New `scripts/MenuUI.cs` — `MenuUI.AddBackButton(screen, font,
  handler)` is now the only way a tab makes its Back button. The placement,
  size, font size and colours live there, so a tab **cannot** pick its own.
- **Two rules a caller must follow** (both documented at the call site):
  1. Pass the **screen root**, not a flow container. Anchoring to the root is
     the whole mechanism — it is what pins the button to the same pixel
     regardless of how that tab lays out its content.
  2. Call it **last** in the tab's UI build, so it draws over the background
     and the flow layout.
- **Gotcha worth remembering**: pulling the button out of PlayGameScreen's
  `topRow` HBox shifted the centred "FIND MATCH" title left, because the title
  is `ExpandFill + Center` and was being balanced by the button on one side and
  the gold label on the other. Fixed with a `MenuUI.BackW`-wide spacer holding
  the vacated slot. Any future tab that removes a Back from a flow row needs
  the same treatment.
- **Text is left-aligned**, not centred, inside the 140px hit box — centring
  indents the glyphs ~45px and the corner inset stops matching `CornerPad`.
  The oversized box stays for the bigger click target.
- **Verified**: captured all three tabs, then cropped the top-left 220x110 of
  each and hashed it — **byte-identical across all three**. Ink bbox
  x[36..79] y[48..65], brightest pixel (217, 41, 41). Screenshots in
  `screenshots/backbtn/`.
- **Deliberately out of scope**: `PauseMenu` / `SettingsPanel` Back+Close (small
  centred cards — a screen-anchored corner would put the button outside the
  card), TitleScreen's "Exit" (quits the app, not a tab back), and
  PlayGameScreen's "Back to menu" in the connection-failure row (a failure
  action, though it now duplicates the persistent top-left Back — worth
  revisiting).

## 2026-08-27 — Arena grass: full carpet, ground no longer visible

- **What**: The arena turf was sparse enough to see floor between blades. Made
  it read as a closed carpet, and measured the cost rather than guessing.
- **Coverage changes** (`scripts/GrassField.cs`):
  - Blades widened (`0.07 -> 0.095` half-width, fragment silhouette
    `w 0.20 -> 0.27`, taper start `0.35 -> 0.42`) and heightened
    (`BladeHeight 0.42 -> 0.60`). **Bigger blades are free coverage** — same
    instance count, same triangle count, same single draw call. Always exhaust
    this before raising density.
  - `BladeCount 60000 -> 110000` only after the above.
  - Root AO softened (`0.50+0.50*v_h -> 0.58+0.42*v_h`) so the dense mat does
    not read as a black ring at ground level.
- **Floor changes** (`scripts/ArenaBuilder.cs`): the floor is under the blades
  now, so it only has to sell "shaded turf" in the gaps —
  `AlbedoColor = (0.40, 0.52, 0.32)` tint + `Uv1Scale 0.12 -> 0.30`
  (8.3m repeat -> 3.3m, so the photo shows blade-scale detail instead of a
  stretched blur). Procedural fallback shader darkened to match. **This tint is
  what makes the distance-faded far edge still read as grass** — beyond
  `DistFadeEnd` (50m) there are no blades at all, only floor.
- **Grass disc pushed out**: `Radius - 2.5f -> Radius - 1.6f` (27.5m -> 28.4m).
  The skirt's inner face sits at ~29.1m at the face centres, so blades reach the
  shelf base without poking through. Do not go past ~28.6.
- **Perf paid for itself**: the vertex shader now early-outs on root verts
  (`if (h > 0.001)`). Every sway term is scaled by `h`, so root verts were
  displacing by exactly zero after paying for two `vn2` noise evaluations and a
  bend-map sample. Roots are **half** of all blade verts.
- **Measured** (viewport GPU render time via
  `RenderingServer.ViewportGetMeasuredRenderTimeGpu`, worst-case framing —
  eye height, orbiting, whole arena on screen; vsync on this box pins FPS at
  143 so frame rate cannot measure this):

  | scene | GPU ms | grass share | draw calls |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | arena, grass removed | 2.97 | — | 239 |
  | **before** (60k, old shader) | 5.18 | 2.21 | 240 |
  | **after** (110k, new shader) | 5.87 | 2.90 | 240 |
  | 160k (rejected) | 6.94 | 3.97 | 240 |

  **+0.69 ms/frame for 83% more blades** — the root early-out ate most of it.
  Draw calls unchanged (MultiMesh is one call regardless of instance count), CPU
  unchanged at ~1.0 ms. Budget is 16.7 ms @ 60fps, 6.9 ms @ 144fps.
- **If more density is ever wanted**: 160k is still only 6.9 ms and was rejected
  on looks, not cost. The real ceiling here is fill rate from `cull_disabled` +
  `ALPHA_SCISSOR`, not vertex count.
- **Verified**: `test/TestArena.tscn` 6-frame capture ->
  `screenshots/grassfull/`. Frames 1 and 3 show a closed carpet to the shelf
  bases. Frame 0's extreme close-up is the pre-existing harness quirk
  (camera renders before the waypoint settles), not a regression.

