## Arena vertical tier stacking (`ArenaBuilder.Tiers`)

Stacked the arena's shelf + column pattern vertically 3× (36m wall → 108m),
built cheap-first, then measured whether it actually strains the engine.

### What changed (`scripts/ArenaBuilder.cs`)

- `Tiers` / `TierUnitH` / `ShelfBandH` constants. `ArenaWallH` is now
  `TierUnitH * Tiers`. **`Tiers = 1` reproduces the pre-stacking arena
  exactly** — that identity is the A/B baseline and the correctness check on
  the refactor; don't let it drift.
- `AddBookshelfRing` / `AddShelfCollision` / `AddSecondTier` take
  `(baseY, tier)` and build in tier-local Y; `Build()` loops them.
- **MultiMesh prop batching.** GLB props (bookrows, columns, balustrades) go
  into one `MultiMeshInstance3D` per (prop, tier) instead of one node each:
  ~1400 placements at Tiers=3 → ~9 draw calls. Batched per-tier, not
  globally, so each batch keeps a tight AABB — that preserves frustum culling
  of tiers you aren't looking at and keeps the gl_compatibility per-object
  light list (`max_lights_per_object=32`) scoped to that tier's own lights.
- Merged shelf meshes are also committed per-tier, same reasoning.
- `PropMesh()` / `PropStretch()` cache the GLB mesh and its AABB-derived scale
  once per prop name, removing ~1400 `Instantiate()` + `GetLocalAabb()` calls
  from arena build.
- Lights are **not** tripled 1:1 — tier 0 keeps the full `Sides/2` torch ring,
  upper tiers get half density. Under gl_compatibility's per-object forward
  lighting, light count feeds straight into fragment cost.
- Used `Basis.ScaledLocal`, not `Basis.Scaled`, when composing batch
  transforms: a Node3D composes as Rotation × Scale (local), and `Scaled`
  pre-multiplies, which would shear the non-uniformly stretched balustrade.

### Measurement (`test/TestArenaPerf.cs` + `.tscn`)

Arena-only harness. Run: `godot --path . res://test/TestArenaPerf.tscn -- xN`
(`empty` = control, `xN` = N arenas side by side).

**Trap this machine hit:** `WindowSetVsyncMode(Disabled)` *reports* Disabled
and `Engine.MaxFps = 0`, but the driver still forces a hard 144Hz cap — an
**empty scene with 0 draw calls also reports 6.94ms/144fps**. Always run the
`empty` control before believing any frame time here. To get off the cap the
harness replicates the arena until frame time moves.

Results (600 sampled frames after 180 warmup, player-eye orbit at floor level):

| copies | Tiers=1 | Tiers=3 | delta |
|--------|---------|---------|-------|
| 1      | 6.96 ms (capped) | 6.96 ms (capped) | — |
| 4      | 8.61 ms | 9.27 ms | +0.66 ms |
| 8      | 10.02 ms | 10.85 ms | +0.83 ms |
| 16     | 12.45 ms | 16.06 ms | +3.61 ms |

**Verdict: tripling costs roughly 0.1–0.25 ms/frame per arena** (~1% of a
60fps budget). A single tripled arena does not measurably move frame time —
it stays pinned at the refresh cap. Draw calls stayed **12** and primitives
**466k** at both tier counts (frustum culling means you never see more at
once); node count 182 → 280; arena build +112ms one-time.

Note the frame-time growth tracks **node count**, not draw calls or
primitives (both plateaued at 22/931k from x4 on) — i.e. this renderer is
CPU-side per-object bound, which is exactly what the MultiMesh batching
attacks.

